data.content:
Since `TimestampFormatter` is static but `TimeProvider` comes from DI, you have a few options:

**Option 1 – Make it non-static (recommended)**

Convert to a regular class so DI can inject `TimeProvider`:

```csharp
public class TimestampFormatter(TimeProvider timeProvider)
{
    public string Now() => timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

Register it: `services.AddSingleton<TimestampFormatter>();`

**Option 2 – Accept `TimeProvider` as a parameter (keep static)**

```csharp
public static class TimestampFormatter
{
    public static string Now(TimeProvider timeProvider)
        => timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

**Option 3 – Default to `TimeProvider.System` with optional override**

```csharp
public static class TimestampFormatter
{
    public static string Now(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => (timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System).GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

---

**Recommendation:** Option 1 is the cleanest. Static classes and DI don't mix well—converting to an injectable singleton gives you proper testability and aligns with the DI-registered `TimeProvider`. Want me to apply one of these?
